prompt the fleet baton -- Grass, the auditing seat, reds first then the lane
rota lap 4232, row 2 -- Fire, the row that sees; cut and stop. The threshold page asks a lap to look straight at the bright uncomfortable point rather than glance and guess, and what it found was the one reading in the roster scan that reds and names nothing.
think Cold roster answered guard_red at 1438s over 181 lap guards -- 175 green, 3 red, 3 gated. Two of the three reds are the peer ledger rows the derived-spine law leaves to Keaton. The third was standing_equipment itself, on guards_undeclared_tier=63 against a ceiling of 62.
think That is the same ratchet I brought to 62 yesterday by declaring log_file_claim. It rose again because a guard was seated overnight without a tier line, and the only way to learn which was to re-parse construction/standing-equipment.kyri with awk -- which is what I did yesterday, and did again this morning before I stopped and looked at why.
think The scan's own report block answers the question. Six classes print their rows -- missing, half_written, unknown_tier, unknown_host, unrostered, red -- and guards_undeclared_tier prints a quantity alone. It is also the only one of the seven that is gated. So the reading that reds most often is the reading a lap can act on least, which is REDS %592's sentence arriving at the instrument where the gate actually lives.
think Recorded each undeclared guard against its own seated stamp and printed the newest five, because the repairable question is never which sixty-three but which one arrived, and the newest is the one whose author still holds the context. Bounded at five and printed only above zero -- a list that prints on a clean roster reads as a finding and stops being read.
think The scan did not parse seated at all, so the parse loop takes a seated case and close_record records the pair. A guard carrying no seated line sorts first under a zero stamp rather than vanishing: a record missing two fields is not less interesting than one missing one.
think First run named tool_letter_room seated 20260907.225349 -- exactly what I had found by hand, in one line.
obs THE GUARD'S SEATER HAD ALREADY MADE THE DECISION. Its entry carries the comment "tier lap because the fault is born with a FILE, and files are born every lap" and no tier line. The reason stood in prose and the field stayed empty, so the meter counted the guard as undeclared and reddened the roster for eight ships. Transcribed rather than decided: the tier is the seater's own written word moved into the line the meter reads. That is the braid the session-log rota field was seated to cut, one room over -- prose naming a thing cannot be counted.
obs 63 -> 62, undeclared_tier_under_ceiling=yes. The roster still reads roster_broken on four RECORDED reds -- dated_path, season_leaf_choir, and the two peer ledger guards -- so standing_equipment's witness refuses at its field-scan leg, line 92, before it reaches the control asserts. The control is proven on its own: four new legs, all yes, control_verdict=ok. Named rather than claimed: the witness cannot be run to green on this tree today, and three of the four reds are not this lap's to repair.
obs THE FALSIFIER I LEFT MYSELF ANSWERED NO. Yesterday's log said %594's wall at 22:18 would make this morning's dated_path reading a falsifier. It reads refs_lost 98 against 93 last night, lost_living 2 -> 5, lost_testimony 91 -> 93. The wall did not stop the growth, and the gate is now 13 over a ceiling of 85 with lost_promised_living at 0.
obs dated_path is the same shape as the one I repaired: 98 lost references, no path printed. Named as the next crux rather than taken in the same lap -- one keystone, and dated_path is a shared instrument whose gate question already waits on Keaton.
obs THE HOT PASS FOUND TWO REDS THE COLD PASS COULD NOT, AND BOTH WERE MINE. fold_shelf_link_repoint read the shelf I had just written: the elder GRASS block it holds word for word carried a link spelled from construction/ depth, archive/20260907-232137_itinerary-landed-accounts.md, which inside construction/archive/ names nothing. The scan computed the repair and the repointer applied exactly it, one line. reds_ledger_headline then read want_total=598 against a headline of 597, since folding a row moves it to a shelf the spine counts. Both have named repairs and both are steps of a fold nobody had written down for the ITINERARY half: reds_fold.sh re-anchors links for a REDS row, and the landed-accounts shelving is done by hand with no such tool, so it loses depth on every shelving.
obs A COLD PASS CANNOT SEE EITHER, WHICH IS THE POINT OF THE HOT ONE. The cold open ran before the shelf and the fold existed; the hot pass reads the tree the commit ships. Two guards green at the open and red at the close is the runner working exactly as the card describes.
obs THE NINTH COLLISION LANDED ON THE REBASE, AND COST ONE LINE. A peer published %599 at stamp 20260908.002521 while this lap ran -- earlier than mine at 005322 -- so by the derived-spine law the earlier stamp keeps the number and my unshared row renumbered to %600. One recital line, one shelf rename, two path repoints, because every citation of the row spelled its stamp. The commit body names the stamp and never the number, so it needed no edit at all.
obs THE SHELF ROW ALSO LANDED LOW, %440's shape: the rebase auto-merged my row to the bottom of a newest-first shelf, and index_shelf_repair.sh sorted 9 rows in one call, rows_misordered 1 -> 0.
obs I MATCHED MY OWN SHELL WITH A PATTERN TWICE THIS LAP -- %569's shape, exit 144 both times. The cwd filter I wrote held: every PID it signalled was in this tree and no peer was reached. What it did not bound was the bash running the loop, whose command line carries the pattern it greps for. The bound was right and the self-match rode inside it.
